@prefix bfo: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix ontara-dom: <https://ontara.dev/ontology/domain/> .
@prefix ontara-rsn: <https://ontara.dev/ontology/reasoning/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .


# =============================================================================
# Ontology declaration
# =============================================================================

ontara-rsn: a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Ontara Reasoning Vocabulary"@en ;
    dcterms:created "2026-04-06"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """OWL 2 DL representation of the reasoning metamodel for the Ontara platform.
Grounded in BFO 2020 via IAO and aligned with W3C PROV-O via dual subclassing
(S147-D4). Designed across the Institutionalised Reasoning paper (Session 146)
and the Coordinate Framework Revisited paper (Session 147). All fifteen design
decisions (S146-D1 to D8, S147-D1 to D7) confirmed Session 148.

This module follows the pattern established by ontara-governance.ttl — hand-authored,
OWL-authoritative (B29). The reasoning metamodel is an extension of the Service
Meta Model (S146-D1), not a third meta model.

Phase 1 Step 1.1 (Session 150): PROV-O import and dual subclassing foundation.
Phase 1 Step 1.2 (Session 151): Core reasoning classes — ReasoningContext,
  Goal, Obstacle, Measure, Decision, Plan, Constraint hierarchy (Hard, Soft,
  Graded), KnowledgeSource, Heuristic, ReasoningComponent. Properties:
  hasContext, hasGoal, hasConstraint, producedDecision, hasDecisionMode,
  hasObstacle, hasMeasure, hasPlan, hasKnowledgeSource, plus domain/range."""@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "Session 155 — Phase 2: Heuristic packs (6 subtypes + HeuristicPack), decision mode routing (4 Cynefin individuals), constraint satisfaction structures (CombinationAlgebra with 4 algebras)"@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 1: Dual-Subclassed Foundation Classes (S147-D4)
# =============================================================================
#
# Established Session 150 (Step 1.1). Three classes with dual BFO + PROV-O
# parentage forming the provenance bridge.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ReasoningActivity — the core reasoning episode
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ReasoningActivity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Activity"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:BFO_0000015 ,
                    prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:comment """A single reasoning episode — a bounded process in which knowledge sources
are consulted, constraints evaluated, and a decision or claim produced. Dual
subclassing: BFO process (ontological grounding) and PROV-O Activity
(provenance interoperability). Every ReasoningActivity has a context, may
use entities, and produces decisions or claims with evidence trails.

BFO grounding: process (BFO_0000015) — an entity that unfolds in time.
PROV-O alignment: Activity — something that occurs over a period of time
and acts upon or with entities."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A bounded reasoning episode that consults knowledge sources, evaluates
constraints, and produces decisions or claims. The fundamental unit of
reasoning provenance in the Ontara platform."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claim — an assertion produced by reasoning
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Claim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Claim"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ,
                    prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:comment """An assertion produced by a ReasoningActivity. Dual subclassing: IAO
information content entity (ontological grounding — a claim is about
something and is the output of a cognitive or computational process)
and PROV-O Entity (provenance interoperability — a claim is a thing
with provenance that can be attributed, derived, and traced).

The evidence architecture (SEPIO pattern, S146-D7) will link Claims
to EvidenceLines and ConfidenceAssessments in Step 1.3.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030).
PROV-O alignment: Entity — a thing with fixed aspects."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An assertion with provenance — the output of a reasoning episode,
supported by evidence lines and carrying a confidence assessment
with a declared interpretive frame."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ReasoningAgent — an entity that performs reasoning
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ReasoningAgent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Agent"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:BFO_0000040 ,
                    prov:Agent ;
    rdfs:comment """An entity that bears responsibility for a reasoning activity — a human
practitioner, a software component, or a composite of both. Dual
subclassing: BFO material entity (ontological grounding) and PROV-O
Agent (provenance interoperability).

BFO grounding: material entity (BFO_0000040).
PROV-O alignment: Agent — something that bears responsibility for
an activity taking place."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An entity (human, software, or composite) that bears responsibility
for reasoning activities and their outputs."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 2: Core Reasoning Classes (Step 1.2)
# =============================================================================
#
# These classes define the abstract reasoning hierarchy per the Stage 7 plan
# §4.1.2. BFO grounding follows the coordinate framework revisited paper
# (Session 147) — in particular, constraint geometry (§7, S147-D3) and the
# Region taxonomy (§6, S147-D2) determine BFO category assignments.
#
# Key BFO grounding principle for Step 1.2: reasoning concepts that specify
# what should happen (goals, constraints, plans) are directive or prescriptive
# information entities. Concepts that record what has happened or been observed
# (decisions, measures, obstacles) are descriptive information entities.
# Both families are generically dependent continuants (information).
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ReasoningContext — the conditions under which reasoning occurs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Context"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:BFO_0000031 ;
    rdfs:comment """The context within which a reasoning activity occurs. Captures the
domain position, decision mode, available knowledge sources, active
constraints, and applicable goals. A ReasoningContext is generically
dependent — it is information about the conditions of reasoning, not
the reasoning itself.

The decision mode classification (S146-D5) is a property of
ReasoningContext: clear, complicated, complex, or chaotic (Cynefin
domain classification as ClassificationRegion in coordinate space,
per S147-D2). Different decision modes activate different reasoning
components.

BFO grounding: generically dependent continuant (BFO_0000031) —
context information depends on some bearer for its existence."""@en ;
    skos:definition """The set of conditions — domain position, decision mode, goals,
constraints, and knowledge sources — under which a reasoning
activity is conducted."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ReasoningComponent — abstract type for reasoning capabilities
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ReasoningComponent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Component"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:BFO_0000031 ;
    rdfs:comment """Abstract type for typed reasoning capabilities that can be activated
within a ReasoningContext. Concrete subtypes include deterministic
rule engines (Tier 1), inspectable logic components (Tier 2),
structured probabilistic models (new category per S147-D5), and
opaque probabilistic models (Tier 3, advisory only).

ReasoningComponents are specifications — information about what kind
of reasoning is available and how it operates. They are not processes
themselves; they are activated by ReasoningActivities.

BFO grounding: generically dependent continuant (BFO_0000031) —
a specification that is concretised in a reasoning activity."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An abstract specification of a typed reasoning capability. Concrete
subtypes define specific reasoning regimes that can be activated
within a reasoning context."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Goal — an intentional target referencing a GoalRegion
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Goal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Goal"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000033 ;
    rdfs:comment """An intentional target — a directive specifying what the system is trying
to reach or maintain. In coordinate-framework terms (S147-D2, S146-D4),
a Goal references a GoalRegion in coordinate space: a region the system
is trying to reach or remain within. GoalRegions may be vague, multi-
dimensional, and temporally evolving. Connected to valence (L7) — goal
regions are valence anchors.

Goal refinement is decomposition along coordinate axes. Goal-seeking
computation (L9) is pathfinding toward a GoalRegion through the
constrained coordinate space (S147-D3).

BFO grounding: directive information entity (IAO_0000033) — a Goal
directs the system toward a desired state, analogous to how governance
obligations (ontara-gov:DeonticDirective) direct compliance. The
parallel with the governance vocabulary is deliberate: goals and
obligations are both directive information entities with different
normative force."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An intentional target referencing a GoalRegion in coordinate space.
Specifies what the system is trying to reach or maintain, with
possible decomposition along coordinate axes."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Obstacle — a condition constraining trajectories toward a goal
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Obstacle a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Obstacle"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """A condition that constrains or impedes trajectories toward a Goal.
An Obstacle is descriptive information — it records a fact about
the environment that makes goal attainment harder. In coordinate-
framework terms, an Obstacle identifies a region or condition in the
coordinate space that the system must work around or overcome.

Distinguished from Constraint: an Obstacle is a discovered condition
in the environment; a Constraint is a declared rule or preference.
An Obstacle may give rise to a Constraint (the discovery of a
capacity limit becomes a HardConstraint on scheduling), but they
are ontologically distinct.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — an
Obstacle is information about a condition in the environment."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A condition that constrains or impedes trajectories toward a goal,
identified through observation or analysis of the environment."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measure — a projection onto a coordinate axis
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Measure a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """A projection onto a specific coordinate axis for evaluation. A Measure
defines how progress toward a Goal or compliance with a Constraint is
assessed — it selects one or more axes from the coordinate space and
specifies the assessment semantics (threshold, range, trend, rate).

In coordinate-framework terms (A12), a Measure is a named projection:
it takes a multi-dimensional coordinate position and extracts a scalar
or structured value from a specific axis or combination of axes.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a Measure
is information about how to evaluate a dimension of the coordinate
space."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A projection onto a coordinate axis for evaluation — defines how
progress, compliance, or state is assessed along a specific
dimension of the coordinate space."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decision — a choice among alternatives
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Decision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Decision"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ,
                    prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:comment """A choice among alternatives, produced by a ReasoningActivity. A Decision
records which alternative was selected, the reasoning context in which
the choice was made, and the justification. In coordinate-framework
terms, a Decision selects a trajectory through the coordinate space
from among the feasible alternatives.

Dual subclassing with prov:Entity provides provenance traceability:
every Decision records who made it, when, using what knowledge, and
with what evidence basis.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a Decision
is a recorded choice, information about what was decided.
PROV-O alignment: Entity — a thing with provenance."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A choice among alternatives, produced by a reasoning activity,
with full provenance traceability."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plan — a sequence of intended actions toward a Goal
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Plan a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Plan"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000104 ;
    rdfs:comment """A sequence of intended actions toward a Goal. In coordinate-framework
terms, a Plan specifies a trajectory through the coordinate space
from the current position toward a GoalRegion, accounting for
Constraints and Obstacles.

BFO grounding: plan specification (IAO_0000104) — IAO defines plan
specification as 'an information content entity whose concretization
indicates to a performer what actions should be taken.' This is the
precise BFO/IAO category for Plans."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A sequence of intended actions specifying a trajectory through the
coordinate space toward a goal, accounting for constraints and
obstacles."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Constraint — abstract constraint type (S146-D8, S147-D3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BFO grounding rationale (resolved Session 151):
#
# The prep note for this session posed the question: are constraints
# GenericallyDependentContinuant (like information) or RealizableEntity
# (like dispositions)?
#
# Answer: Constraints are INFORMATION. The coordinate framework revisited
# paper (Session 147 §7) establishes constraint geometry: constraints
# describe what is permissible (HardConstraint = boundary), preferred
# (SoftConstraint = cost surface), or true to a degree (GradedRule =
# truth-value surface). These are all specifications — things that can
# be written down, communicated, and shared. They are generically
# dependent continuants, specifically information content entities.
#
# This aligns with the governance ontology pattern where DeonticDirective
# (ontara-gov:) is already an IAO directive information entity subclass.
# Governance obligations ARE hard constraints in the reasoning metamodel's
# terms (§7.1 of the coordinate framework revisited paper).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Constraint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """Abstract constraint type. In the reasoning metamodel, constraints shape
the coordinate space through which reasoning navigates. The three-way
distinction (S146-D8, grounded by S147-D3): HardConstraint (boundary),
SoftConstraint (cost surface), GradedRule (truth-value surface).

Constraints are information — they specify what is permissible,
preferred, or true. They are not dispositions of material entities.
A constraint can be written down, communicated, and shared across
contexts. This parallels the governance vocabulary where
ontara-gov:DeonticDirective is also an IAO subclass.

The governance–reasoning connection: governance obligations are
HardConstraints that define NormativeRegions (§7.1 of the coordinate
framework revisited paper). This alignment is established in Step 1.7.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a
constraint is information about what is permissible, preferred,
or true in the coordinate space."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A specification that shapes the coordinate space through which
reasoning navigates. Subtyped into HardConstraint (boundary),
SoftConstraint (cost surface), and GradedRule (truth-value
surface)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HardConstraint — NormativeRegion boundary (S147-D3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:HardConstraint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Hard Constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:comment """A constraint defining a boundary that cannot be violated. In coordinate-
framework terms (S147-D3), a HardConstraint defines a NormativeRegion
or StaticBoundary in the coordinate space. The constraint is satisfied
when the entity's position is within the region; it is violated when
the position crosses the boundary.

Governance obligations from the deontic vocabulary (B30–B35) are
HardConstraints that define NormativeRegions. Safety constraints from
STAMP/STPA (Phase 3) will define safety-critical NormativeRegions
whose boundaries must never be crossed.

Examples: CQC Regulation 12 compliance boundary, therapeutic range
limits, equipment capacity ceiling, staffing minimum."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A constraint defining a boundary in the coordinate space that
cannot be violated — crossing the boundary is a failure event
with governance or safety consequences."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SoftConstraint — ScalarField cost/preference surface (S147-D3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:SoftConstraint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Soft Constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:comment """A constraint defining a continuous cost or preference landscape over
the coordinate space. Every point in the space has a cost associated
with it; the system seeks to minimise cost or maximise preference.
A SoftConstraint shapes trajectories rather than bounding them.

In coordinate-framework terms (S147-D3), a SoftConstraint is a
ScalarField. The semiring framework provides the algebraic structure:
min+ for costs, max× for preferences (from the probabilistic
reasoning research).

Phase 2 (Step 2.3) will elaborate semiring properties for
SoftConstraints — combination operators, composition rules.

Examples: resource allocation preferences, scheduling cost, patient
travel distance, staff continuity preference."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A constraint defining a cost or preference surface over the
coordinate space. Shapes trajectories toward lower-cost paths
rather than imposing sharp boundaries."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GradedRule — ScalarField truth-value surface (S147-D3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:GradedRule a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Graded Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:comment """A constraint assigning a truth value in [0,1] to every point in the
coordinate space, representing the degree to which a business rule
or heuristic applies. PSL (Probabilistic Soft Logic) provides the
computational semantics: graded rules combine via convex optimisation
to find the most probable truth-value assignment.

In coordinate-framework terms (S147-D3), a GradedRule is a ScalarField
over the truth-value domain. Similar to SoftConstraint but with
different semantics: degree of applicability rather than cost.

Phase 2 (Step 2.3) will elaborate truth-value range and composition
rules for GradedRules.

Examples: degree-of-suitability for a treatment approach, strength
of clinical indication, confidence in a diagnostic criterion."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A constraint assigning a truth value in [0,1] to every point in
the coordinate space, representing the degree to which a rule
applies."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KnowledgeSource — abstract type for knowledge inputs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:KnowledgeSource a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Source"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """An abstract type for knowledge inputs to reasoning. KnowledgeSources
are consulted by ReasoningActivities (via prov:used) and contribute
to the production of Claims and Decisions. Concrete subtypes include
clinical guidelines, regulatory instruments, population data sets,
expert knowledge, and heuristic collections.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a
KnowledgeSource is information that can be consulted."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A source of knowledge consulted during a reasoning activity.
Abstract type — concrete subtypes specify the kind of knowledge
and its provenance."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Heuristic — a declarative heuristic (S146-D6)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:Heuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:KnowledgeSource ;
    rdfs:comment """A declarative heuristic — a practical rule of thumb that guides
reasoning without being formally mandatory. Heuristics are OWL
individuals with typed properties (S146-D6) declaring applicability
conditions, ordering logic, and authority basis.

In coordinate-framework terms (§7.4 of the coordinate framework
revisited paper), heuristics are navigation strategies — rules for
choosing among the many possible paths that satisfy the constraints.
Heuristics expressed geometrically become formally comparable,
composable, and overrideable (the override is a ReasoningActivity
with provenance).

Phase 2 (Step 2.1) will elaborate the heuristic type hierarchy
and HeuristicPack structure.

Examples: 'prefer same-barista continuity' (Cafe), 'high-temperature
washes first' (Suds), 'prefer morning appointments for elderly
patients' (clinical)."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A declarative heuristic — a practical navigation strategy that
guides reasoning along preferred paths through the coordinate
space without imposing formal boundaries."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 3: Object Properties (Step 1.2)
# =============================================================================
#
# These properties connect the core reasoning classes. Domain and range
# declarations are stated explicitly. OWL characteristics (functional,
# inverse functional) are declared where appropriate.
#
# PROV-O properties (prov:used, prov:wasGeneratedBy, etc.) are inherited
# via dual subclassing and not re-declared here.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasContext — links ReasoningActivity to ReasoningContext
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasContext a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has context"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningActivity ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningActivity to the ReasoningContext in which it
was conducted. Every reasoning episode occurs within a specific
context that determines available knowledge, active constraints,
applicable goals, and the decision mode."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasGoal — links ReasoningContext to Goal
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasGoal a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has goal"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Goal ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningContext to the Goal(s) that the reasoning is
directed toward. A context may have multiple goals (which may
be complementary, competing, or hierarchically decomposed)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasConstraint — links ReasoningContext to Constraint
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasConstraint a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningContext to the Constraint(s) that shape the
reasoning. Constraints include hard boundaries (HardConstraint),
cost surfaces (SoftConstraint), and truth-value surfaces
(GradedRule)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasObstacle — links ReasoningContext to Obstacle
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasObstacle a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has obstacle"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Obstacle ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningContext to Obstacle(s) identified as impeding
progress toward the context's goals."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasMeasure — links Goal to Measure
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasMeasure a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has measure"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Goal ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Measure ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Goal to the Measure(s) used to assess progress toward it.
Each Measure is a named projection onto a coordinate axis,
defining how attainment of the goal is evaluated."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# producedDecision — links ReasoningActivity to Decision
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:producedDecision a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "produced decision"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningActivity ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Decision ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningActivity to the Decision it produced. Complements
prov:wasGeneratedBy (which links from the Decision back to the
Activity). A ReasoningActivity may produce zero or more Decisions
(zero if the activity produces Claims but no decision)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasDecisionMode — links ReasoningContext to decision mode classification
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasDecisionMode a owl:ObjectProperty ,
                           owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has decision mode"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningContext to its decision mode classification (S146-D5).
Functional — a context has exactly one active decision mode. The
decision mode determines which ReasoningComponents are activated.

Phase 2 (Step 2.2) will elaborate DecisionMode with Cynefin domain
mapping and routing rules."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasPlan — links Decision to Plan
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasPlan a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has plan"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Decision ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Plan ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Decision to the Plan that implements it. A Decision may
have zero plans (immediate action) or one plan (a structured
sequence of intended actions toward a goal)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasKnowledgeSource — links ReasoningContext to KnowledgeSource
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasKnowledgeSource a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has knowledge source"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:KnowledgeSource ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ReasoningContext to the KnowledgeSource(s) available for
consultation during reasoning. This includes guidelines, regulatory
instruments, heuristics, and any other information inputs."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 4: DecisionMode Enumeration (placeholder for Phase 2)
# =============================================================================
#
# DecisionMode is referenced by hasDecisionMode. It will be elaborated in
# Phase 2 Step 2.2 with Cynefin domain mapping and routing rules. For now,
# it exists as a minimal class to support the property declaration.
#
# =============================================================================

ontara-rsn:DecisionMode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Decision Mode"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """Classification of the reasoning context's problem domain (S146-D5).
Determines which ReasoningComponents are activated. Phase 2 will
elaborate with Cynefin domain mapping (clear, complicated, complex,
chaotic) as ClassificationRegion instances (S147-D2).

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a
classification is information about the nature of the problem."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A classification of the reasoning context's problem domain that
determines which reasoning components are activated."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# PROV-O Provenance Properties — Usage Notes
# =============================================================================
#
# Key PROV-O properties used by ReasoningActivity:
#   prov:used              — knowledge sources consulted
#   prov:wasAssociatedWith — agent(s) responsible
#   prov:wasInformedBy     — prior reasoning activities in a chain
#   prov:startedAtTime     — when reasoning began
#   prov:endedAtTime       — when reasoning concluded
#
# Key PROV-O properties used by Claim and Decision:
#   prov:wasGeneratedBy    — the ReasoningActivity that produced it
#   prov:wasAttributedTo   — the ReasoningAgent responsible
#   prov:wasDerivedFrom    — prior claims or entities it builds on
#   prov:generatedAtTime   — when the entity was produced
#
# These are inherited from prov-core.ttl via dual subclassing and not
# re-declared. ReasoningActivity IS a prov:Activity; Claim and Decision
# ARE prov:Entities. All PROV-O properties apply directly.
#
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 5: Evidence Architecture (Step 1.3, SEPIO pattern, S146-D7)
# =============================================================================
#
# The evidence architecture follows the SEPIO (Scientific Evidence and
# Provenance Information Ontology) pattern, adapted for Ontara. Every Claim
# is supported by EvidenceLines, each composed of EvidenceItems. Every Claim
# carries a ConfidenceAssessment with a declared interpretive frame.
#
# The three interpretive frames (S147-D1, stable since Session 46):
#   - Probability: "70% likelihood" — combined via Bayesian updating
#   - Fuzzy membership: "moderately suitable" — combined via fuzzy operators
#   - Preference/cost: "preferred at strength 0.7" — combined via semiring
#
# Provenance note: Claim already subclasses prov:Entity (Step 1.1), so
# prov:wasGeneratedBy links a Claim to the ReasoningActivity that produced
# it. We do not create a parallel wasProducedBy property — the PROV-O
# property is the correct one. The plan's "wasProducedBy" intent is
# fulfilled by prov:wasGeneratedBy via dual subclassing.
#
# Connection to E026 (GraphRAG, Session 151): these evidence chains are
# exactly the structures a future GraphRAG retrieval layer would traverse.
# Ensuring they are complete and SPARQL-queryable serves both the immediate
# reasoning architecture and the future LLM-grounding use case.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EvidenceLine — a line of evidence supporting a Claim
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:EvidenceLine a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence Line"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """A coherent line of evidence supporting a Claim. An EvidenceLine groups
related EvidenceItems into an argument — a reason to believe the Claim.
A Claim may be supported by multiple EvidenceLines (converging evidence
from different sources), and each EvidenceLine may contain multiple
EvidenceItems (specific data points, observations, or references).

The SEPIO pattern separates the assertion (Claim) from its evidential
basis (EvidenceLine) and the individual evidence artifacts (EvidenceItem).
This three-level structure enables: independent assessment of each
evidence line, tracing of which evidence contributed to which conclusion,
and updating of evidence without modifying the Claim itself.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — an
EvidenceLine is information about why a claim is supported."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A coherent line of evidence supporting a Claim, grouping
related evidence items into a structured argument."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EvidenceItem — a specific piece of evidence
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:EvidenceItem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence Item"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """A specific piece of evidence within an EvidenceLine. An EvidenceItem
may be an observation, a measurement, a document reference, a dataset,
a prior Claim, or any other identifiable artifact that contributes to
the evidential basis of a Claim.

EvidenceItems carry their own provenance via PROV-O (they can be
prov:Entities with wasGeneratedBy, wasAttributedTo, etc.) but are
not dual-subclassed at the class level — individual EvidenceItem
instances that need provenance tracing can be typed as both
EvidenceItem and prov:Entity.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a specific
piece of evidence is information."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A specific piece of evidence — an observation, measurement,
document reference, or prior claim — within an evidence line."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ConfidenceAssessment — evidential confidence with interpretive frame
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ConfidenceAssessment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Confidence Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """An assessment of evidential confidence attached to a Claim, with a
declared interpretive frame (S147-D1). The interpretive frame specifies
how the confidence value should be understood and combined:

  - Probability frame: confidence is a probability (0–1), combined via
    Bayesian updating, represented as distributions.
  - Fuzzy membership frame: confidence is a degree of membership (0–1),
    combined via fuzzy operators (max-min, etc.).
  - Preference/cost frame: confidence is a preference weight (0–1),
    combined via semiring operations (min+, max×).

These three frames were first identified in the intrinsic self-knowledge
paper (Session 46) §4.6 and have remained stable across 100+ sessions
and two independent lines of architectural inquiry (comprehension and
reasoning). Their stability validates them as a correct decomposition
of the confidence space.

A ConfidenceAssessment without a declared interpretive frame is
meaningless — the frame determines the algebra. This is enforced
by the hasInterpretiveFrame property.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a confidence
assessment is information about the strength of evidential support."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An assessment of evidential confidence with a declared interpretive
frame (probability, fuzzy membership, or preference weight) that
determines how the confidence value is understood and combined."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# InterpretiveFrame — the three confidence interpretation schemes
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:InterpretiveFrame a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Interpretive Frame"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """The interpretive frame for a ConfidenceAssessment — determines how
the confidence value is understood and combined with other assessments.
Three frames are defined (S147-D1, stable since Session 46):

  - ProbabilityFrame: Bayesian probability semantics
  - FuzzyMembershipFrame: fuzzy set membership semantics
  - PreferenceWeightFrame: semiring preference/cost semantics

Declared as an OWL class with three named individuals rather than as
an enumeration, to allow future extension if additional interpretive
frames emerge (consistent with J3, non-constraining).

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — an
interpretive frame is a specification of how to read a value."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A specification of how a confidence value should be interpreted
and algebraically combined — probability, fuzzy membership, or
preference weight."""@en .


# --- InterpretiveFrame Individuals ---

ontara-rsn:ProbabilityFrame a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             ontara-rsn:InterpretiveFrame ;
    rdfs:label "Probability Frame"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Confidence interpreted as Bayesian probability (0–1). Combined via
Bayesian updating. Represented as distributions over coordinate
space regions (ProbabilityDistribution in the Region taxonomy,
S147-D2)."""@en .

ontara-rsn:FuzzyMembershipFrame a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 ontara-rsn:InterpretiveFrame ;
    rdfs:label "Fuzzy Membership Frame"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Confidence interpreted as fuzzy set membership degree (0–1). Combined
via fuzzy operators (max-min, product-sum). Used for graded
applicability assessments."""@en .

ontara-rsn:PreferenceWeightFrame a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  ontara-rsn:InterpretiveFrame ;
    rdfs:label "Preference Weight Frame"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Confidence interpreted as preference or cost weight (0–1). Combined
via semiring operations (min+, max×). Used for soft constraint
satisfaction and resource allocation preferences."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 6: Evidence Architecture Properties (Step 1.3)
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# supportedBy — links Claim to EvidenceLine
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:supportedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "supported by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Claim ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:EvidenceLine ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Claim to the EvidenceLine(s) that support it. A Claim
may have multiple evidence lines representing converging evidence
from independent sources. The absence of any evidence lines
indicates an unsupported assertion."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasEvidence — links EvidenceLine to EvidenceItem
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasEvidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:EvidenceLine ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:EvidenceItem ;
    rdfs:comment """Links an EvidenceLine to the EvidenceItem(s) that compose it.
Each EvidenceItem is a specific artifact (observation, measurement,
document, prior claim) contributing to the line of evidence."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasConfidence — links Claim to ConfidenceAssessment
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasConfidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has confidence"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Claim ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:ConfidenceAssessment ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Claim to its ConfidenceAssessment. A Claim may have multiple
confidence assessments (e.g. from different evaluation methods or at
different points in time), each with its own interpretive frame."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasInterpretiveFrame — links ConfidenceAssessment to InterpretiveFrame
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasInterpretiveFrame a owl:ObjectProperty ,
                                 owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has interpretive frame"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ConfidenceAssessment ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:InterpretiveFrame ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a ConfidenceAssessment to its InterpretiveFrame. Functional —
each confidence assessment has exactly one interpretive frame that
determines how the confidence value is understood and combined.
A ConfidenceAssessment without a declared frame is meaningless."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasConfidenceValue — the numeric confidence value
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasConfidenceValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ,
                               owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has confidence value"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:ConfidenceAssessment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:comment """The numeric confidence value (typically 0–1) of a ConfidenceAssessment.
The meaning of this value depends on the InterpretiveFrame:
  - ProbabilityFrame: Bayesian probability
  - FuzzyMembershipFrame: degree of membership
  - PreferenceWeightFrame: preference strength

Functional — each assessment has exactly one primary value. For
assessments with bounds (e.g. confidence intervals), additional
data properties (hasLowerBound, hasUpperBound) will be added in
Step 1.4."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# PROV-O Note: wasProducedBy / prov:wasGeneratedBy
# =============================================================================
#
# The Stage 7 plan (§4.1.3) lists "wasProducedBy" as a property linking
# Claim to ReasoningActivity. This is fulfilled by prov:wasGeneratedBy,
# which already applies to Claim via dual subclassing (Claim IS a
# prov:Entity; ReasoningActivity IS a prov:Activity). No parallel
# property is created — using the PROV-O property directly is the
# architecturally correct choice.
#
# Usage: to record that a Claim was produced by a ReasoningActivity,
# assert: :myClaim prov:wasGeneratedBy :myActivity .
#
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# End of Phase 1 Steps 1.2 and 1.3
# =============================================================================
#
# Class count: 20 (3 Step 1.1 + 13 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3)
#   Step 1.1: ReasoningActivity, Claim, ReasoningAgent
#   Step 1.2: ReasoningContext, ReasoningComponent, Goal, Obstacle,
#             Measure, Decision, Plan, Constraint, HardConstraint,
#             SoftConstraint, GradedRule, KnowledgeSource, Heuristic
#             DecisionMode
#   Step 1.3: EvidenceLine, EvidenceItem, ConfidenceAssessment,
#             InterpretiveFrame
#
# Named individuals: 3 (Step 1.3)
#   ProbabilityFrame, FuzzyMembershipFrame, PreferenceWeightFrame
#
# Object property count: 13 (9 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3)
#   Step 1.2: hasContext, hasGoal, hasConstraint, hasObstacle, hasMeasure,
#             producedDecision, hasDecisionMode, hasPlan, hasKnowledgeSource
#   Step 1.3: supportedBy, hasEvidence, hasConfidence, hasInterpretiveFrame
#
# Datatype property count: 1 (Step 1.3)
#   hasConfidenceValue
#
# Next steps:
#   Step 1.5: Cross-domain validation
#   Step 1.6: SPARQL validation suite extension
#   Step 1.7: Integration with governance vocabulary
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 9: Governance Vocabulary Alignment (Step 1.7)
# =============================================================================
#
# This section establishes the connection between the reasoning vocabulary
# (ontara-rsn:) and the governance vocabulary (ontara-gov:). The alignment
# is declared HERE, in the reasoning module, not in the governance module.
#
# Dependency direction (resolved Session 151):
#   ontara-rsn: → ontara-gov:  (reasoning knows about governance)
#   ontara-gov: does NOT import ontara-rsn:
#
# This preserves governance module independence. The governance vocabulary
# was authored Session 126 and predates the reasoning metamodel. Governance
# obligations exist independently of the reasoning infrastructure — they
# existed before Stage 7. The reasoning module is the one that recognises
# governance obligations as a kind of constraint it can reason about.
#
# The alignment has two parts:
#
#   1. Subclass alignment: ontara-gov:Obligation is a subclass of
#      ontara-rsn:HardConstraint. This captures the structural claim
#      from the coordinate framework revisited paper §7.1: governance
#      obligations ARE hard constraints that define NormativeRegion
#      boundaries in the coordinate space.
#
#   2. Semantic connection: governance compliance assessments are
#      structurally Claims with evidence. This is noted as a usage
#      pattern, not a subclass declaration — individual compliance
#      assessment instances can be typed as both governance concepts
#      and ontara-rsn:Claim when evidence traceability is needed.
#
# Note on DeonticDirective covering axiom: the governance module declares
# DeonticDirective as the exact union of Obligation, Permission,
# Prohibition, and RegulatoryPower. Making Obligation a subclass of
# HardConstraint does not violate this axiom — it adds an additional
# superclass to Obligation, which is compatible with OWL 2 DL multiple
# inheritance. An Obligation is simultaneously a DeonticDirective (by
# the governance module's declaration) and a HardConstraint (by this
# module's declaration).
#
# =============================================================================

@prefix ontara-gov: <https://ontara.dev/ontology/governance/> .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Governance → Reasoning alignment: Obligation as HardConstraint
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# From the coordinate framework revisited paper (Session 147) §7.1:
#
#   "Governance obligations from the deontic vocabulary (B30–B35) are
#    HardConstraints that define NormativeRegions. Safety constraints
#    from STAMP/STPA define safety-critical NormativeRegions whose
#    boundaries must never be crossed."
#
# This is not an analogy — it is a structural identity. An obligation
# to monitor within 12 weeks defines a boundary in governance-time
# coordinate space. Crossing it is a compliance failure = constraint
# violation. The HardConstraint class captures exactly this semantics.
#
# By declaring this subclass relationship in the reasoning module
# (not the governance module), we establish that the reasoning
# infrastructure can treat governance obligations as constraints
# without requiring the governance module to know about reasoning.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-gov:Obligation rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:HardConstraint .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Governance → Reasoning alignment: Prohibition as HardConstraint
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# By the same logic, Prohibitions are also hard constraints — they
# define boundaries that must not be crossed (actions that must not
# be performed, states that must not obtain). A Prohibition is a
# HardConstraint whose NormativeRegion boundary, when crossed,
# constitutes a governance violation.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-gov:Prohibition rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:HardConstraint .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Usage note: Compliance assessments as Claims
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A governance compliance assessment — e.g. "CQC Regulation 12 is
# satisfied" — is structurally a Claim:
#   - It is an assertion (Claim)
#   - It is supported by evidence (EvidenceLine → EvidenceItems:
#     audit records, training logs, clinical outcome data)
#   - It carries confidence (ConfidenceAssessment: how certain
#     are we of compliance?)
#   - It has provenance (which ReasoningActivity produced this
#     assessment? who was responsible?)
#
# This alignment is by instance typing, not class subclassing.
# When a governance compliance assessment needs evidence traceability,
# the individual is typed as both a governance concept and an
# ontara-rsn:Claim. This preserves the governance module's
# independence while giving compliance assessments full access to
# the evidence architecture.
#
# Example (not loaded — illustrative):
#
#   :reg12-compliance-2026-Q1 a ontara-rsn:Claim ;
#       rdfs:label "CQC Regulation 12 compliance assessment Q1 2026" ;
#       ontara-rsn:supportedBy :reg12-evidence-line-clinical-outcomes ;
#       ontara-rsn:supportedBy :reg12-evidence-line-staff-training ;
#       ontara-rsn:hasConfidence :reg12-confidence-q1 ;
#       prov:wasGeneratedBy :reg12-assessment-activity-q1 .
#
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# End of Phase 1 Steps 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, and 1.7
# =============================================================================
#
# Class count: 24 (3 Step 1.1 + 13 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3 + 4 Step 1.4)
# Plus 2 cross-module subclass axioms (Step 1.7)
#
#   Step 1.1: ReasoningActivity, Claim, ReasoningAgent
#   Step 1.2: ReasoningContext, ReasoningComponent, Goal, Obstacle,
#             Measure, Decision, Plan, Constraint, HardConstraint,
#             SoftConstraint, GradedRule, KnowledgeSource, Heuristic
#             DecisionMode
#   Step 1.3: EvidenceLine, EvidenceItem, ConfidenceAssessment,
#             InterpretiveFrame
#   Step 1.4: StructuredProbabilisticComponent, BayesianUpdater,
#             RiskCalculator, PrognosticModel, PredictiveAnalytics
#   Step 1.7: ontara-gov:Obligation ⊆ HardConstraint,
#             ontara-gov:Prohibition ⊆ HardConstraint
#
# Named individuals: 3 (Step 1.3)
#   ProbabilityFrame, FuzzyMembershipFrame, PreferenceWeightFrame
#
# Object property count: 15 (9 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3 + 2 Step 1.4)
# Datatype property count: 4 (1 Step 1.3 + 3 Step 1.4)
#
# Remaining:
#   Step 1.5: Cross-domain validation — DONE (Session 151, separate document)
#   Step 1.6: SPARQL validation suite extension — Claude Code task
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 7: Structured Probabilistic Reasoning Types (Step 1.4, S147-D5)
# =============================================================================
#
# The A6 reformulation (S147-D5, coordinate framework revisited §10)
# introduces a new category between Tier 2 (inspectable logic) and Tier 3
# (opaque ML): structured probabilistic models that are deterministic in
# mechanism, inspectable in structure, but probabilistic in output.
#
# These are abstract TYPES — OWL describes the class structure and
# validation metadata requirements. Runtime engines execute the
# computational semantics. Per B29 (authority zones): OWL is authoritative
# for what kinds of probabilistic reasoning exist and what metadata they
# must carry; runtime implementations are authoritative for how the
# computation is actually performed.
#
# All subtypes inherit from ReasoningComponent (BFO_0000031, GDC) —
# they are specifications of reasoning capabilities, not processes.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# StructuredProbabilisticComponent — abstract type (S147-D5)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Structured Probabilistic Component"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:ReasoningComponent ;
    rdfs:comment """Abstract type for validated probabilistic models — the new category
between Tier 2 (inspectable logic) and Tier 3 (opaque ML) per the
A6 reformulation (S147-D5).

Structured probabilistic components share three defining properties:
  1. Deterministic in mechanism — same inputs produce same outputs
  2. Inspectable in structure — assumptions, parameters, and validation
     data are explicit and examinable
  3. Probabilistic in output — the output is a probability, distribution,
     or confidence interval, not a binary decision

In coordinate-framework terms (§10.4 of the coordinate framework
revisited paper): these components characterise the probabilistic
landscape through which deterministic decision paths are planned.
The clinician navigates a deterministic path through a probabilistically
characterised landscape.

Governance requirement: every structured probabilistic component must
carry validation metadata (validation population, performance metrics,
confidence bounds) as a condition of being used as evidence input to
authoritative clinical decisions."""@en ;
    skos:definition """An abstract type for validated probabilistic models that are
deterministic in mechanism, inspectable in structure, and
probabilistic in output."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BayesianUpdater — prior + evidence → posterior
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:BayesianUpdater a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bayesian Updater"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:comment """A structured probabilistic component that takes a prior probability
distribution and evidence, and produces a posterior distribution via
Bayes' theorem. In coordinate-framework terms (reasoning metamodel
§11.6): sequential Bayesian updating is a trajectory through
probability space — the prior defines a ProbabilityDistribution over
a coordinate region, evidence narrows that distribution, and the
posterior is the refined distribution.

Examples: diagnostic probability updating given test results,
treatment response probability given patient characteristics."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A probabilistic component that updates prior probability
distributions with evidence to produce posterior distributions
via Bayes' theorem."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RiskCalculator — validated population-level risk model
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:RiskCalculator a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Risk Calculator"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:BayesianUpdater ;
    rdfs:comment """A BayesianUpdater with explicit validation metadata — a validated
population-level risk model. Distinguished from a generic
BayesianUpdater by the requirement for published validation data:
validation population characteristics, performance metrics
(AUC, sensitivity, specificity, calibration), and confidence
intervals on outputs.

This is the class that accommodates standard evidence-based clinical
tools (QRISK, Framingham, FRAX) that A6 as originally formulated
could not accommodate. The A6 reformulation (S147-D5) permits these
as evidence inputs to clinical decisions, provided validation
metadata is present and the decision path remains deterministic
and inspectable.

Examples: QRISK3 cardiovascular risk calculator, FRAX fracture
risk assessment, validated scoring systems."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A validated population-level risk model with explicit validation
metadata — performance metrics, validation population, and
confidence bounds."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PrognosticModel — time-to-event or trajectory model
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:PrognosticModel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Prognostic Model"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:comment """A structured probabilistic component that models time-to-event or
trajectory outcomes. Produces probability distributions over future
states at specified time horizons. In coordinate-framework terms:
a PrognosticModel projects trajectories forward through the
coordinate space, producing ProbabilityDistributions over future
positions.

Distinguished from BayesianUpdater by the explicit temporal
dimension — a PrognosticModel's output is indexed by time, not
just conditioned on evidence.

Examples: survival curves, disease progression models, treatment
response trajectory predictions."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A probabilistic component that models time-to-event or trajectory
outcomes, producing probability distributions over future states
at specified time horizons."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PredictiveAnalytics — population-level probabilistic analysis
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:PredictiveAnalytics a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Predictive Analytics"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:comment """A structured probabilistic component for population-level analysis
that does not target individual patient outcomes. Produces aggregate
probabilistic assessments: demand forecasting, resource utilisation
projections, population health trend analysis.

Distinguished from RiskCalculator and PrognosticModel by operating
at the population level rather than the individual level. In
coordinate-framework terms: PredictiveAnalytics produces
ProbabilityDistributions over aggregate coordinate-space positions.

Examples: clinic demand forecasting, medication stock prediction,
waiting list trend analysis, population health screening yield."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A probabilistic component for population-level analysis —
demand forecasting, resource projections, and aggregate trend
assessment."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 8: Probabilistic Component Properties (Step 1.4)
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasPrior — links BayesianUpdater to prior distribution
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasPrior a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has prior"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:BayesianUpdater ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Claim ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a BayesianUpdater to its prior probability distribution,
represented as a Claim (an assertion about the prior state of
knowledge). The prior Claim carries its own ConfidenceAssessment
with ProbabilityFrame, and its own evidence chain.

The choice to type the prior as a Claim (rather than a bare
distribution) is deliberate: it ensures the prior has provenance
(where did this prior come from? what evidence supports it?) and
can be traced through the evidence architecture."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasPosterior — links BayesianUpdater to posterior distribution
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasPosterior a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has posterior"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:BayesianUpdater ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Claim ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a BayesianUpdater to its posterior probability distribution,
represented as a Claim. The posterior is the output of Bayesian
updating — the refined distribution after incorporating evidence.
As a Claim, it carries confidence assessment, evidence lines, and
PROV-O provenance (prov:wasGeneratedBy the ReasoningActivity that
executed the update)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasValidationPopulation — validation metadata
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasValidationPopulation a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has validation population"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Describes the population on which the probabilistic component was
validated. Free-text description of cohort characteristics, size,
and representativeness. A governance requirement for any structured
probabilistic component used as evidence in clinical decisions.

Examples: 'UK primary care population aged 25-84, n=2.29 million'
(QRISK3), 'postmenopausal women, NHANES III cohort' (FRAX)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasPerformanceMetric — validation metadata
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasPerformanceMetric a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has performance metric"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Records a performance metric from the component's validation.
Free-text for now — structured metric representation (AUC, C-statistic,
calibration slope, sensitivity, specificity) is a candidate for
future elaboration. Multiple values may be asserted.

Examples: 'AUC 0.84 (95% CI 0.83-0.86)', 'Calibration slope 1.01'."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasConfidenceInterval — bounds on probabilistic output
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasConfidenceInterval a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has confidence interval"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:StructuredProbabilisticComponent ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Records the confidence interval or bounds on the component's
probabilistic output. Free-text for now — structured interval
representation (lower bound, upper bound, confidence level) is
a candidate for future elaboration.

Examples: '95% CI: 9.2%-15.8%', 'Prediction interval: 3-18 months'."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# End of Phase 1 Steps 1.2, 1.3, and 1.4
# =============================================================================
#
# Class count: 24 (3 Step 1.1 + 13 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3 + 4 Step 1.4)
#   Step 1.1: ReasoningActivity, Claim, ReasoningAgent
#   Step 1.2: ReasoningContext, ReasoningComponent, Goal, Obstacle,
#             Measure, Decision, Plan, Constraint, HardConstraint,
#             SoftConstraint, GradedRule, KnowledgeSource, Heuristic
#             DecisionMode
#   Step 1.3: EvidenceLine, EvidenceItem, ConfidenceAssessment,
#             InterpretiveFrame
#   Step 1.4: StructuredProbabilisticComponent, BayesianUpdater,
#             RiskCalculator, PrognosticModel, PredictiveAnalytics
#
# Named individuals: 3 (Step 1.3)
#   ProbabilityFrame, FuzzyMembershipFrame, PreferenceWeightFrame
#
# Object property count: 15 (9 Step 1.2 + 4 Step 1.3 + 2 Step 1.4)
#   Step 1.2: hasContext, hasGoal, hasConstraint, hasObstacle, hasMeasure,
#             producedDecision, hasDecisionMode, hasPlan, hasKnowledgeSource
#   Step 1.3: supportedBy, hasEvidence, hasConfidence, hasInterpretiveFrame
#   Step 1.4: hasPrior, hasPosterior
#
# Datatype property count: 4 (1 Step 1.3 + 3 Step 1.4)
#   Step 1.3: hasConfidenceValue
#   Step 1.4: hasValidationPopulation, hasPerformanceMetric,
#             hasConfidenceInterval
#
# Next steps:
#   Step 1.5: Cross-domain validation
#   Step 1.6: SPARQL validation suite extension
#   Step 1.7: Integration with governance vocabulary
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 10: Heuristic Pack Architecture (Phase 2 Step 2.1)
# =============================================================================
#
# Six Heuristic subclasses covering the primary heuristic families used in
# clinical and service reasoning. Each is grounded in the coordinate framework
# (Session 147) and carries a natural-language description of its ordering
# or selection logic.
#
# A HeuristicPack is a named, versionable collection of heuristics applicable
# to a domain, service line, or regulatory context.
#
# New properties: hasMember, applicableToDomain, applicableToContext,
# overrides, hasAuthorityBasis, hasOrderingLogic.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GoalOrderingHeuristic — task sequencing by value
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:GoalOrderingHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Goal Ordering Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for sequencing goals and tasks. Prioritises prerequisite,
high-risk, irreversible, or high-information-gain tasks first.

Coordinate-framework grounding: prioritise movement along axes with
the highest goal-region proximity gradient — move fastest toward the
region boundaries that matter most."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic that determines goal and task sequencing, prioritising
prerequisite, high-risk, or irreversible actions first."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ResourceHeuristic — scarce-resource preservation
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ResourceHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Resource Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for resource-allocation decisions. Prefers scarce-resource
preservation, continuity-preserving allocations, and local-capacity balancing.

Coordinate-framework grounding: minimise rate of change in resource-axis
dimensions — smooth allocation trajectories preserve capacity."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic that guides resource allocation, preferring
scarce-resource preservation and continuity."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RiskHeuristic — escalation on dangerous combinations
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:RiskHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Risk Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for risk recognition and escalation. Activates on
red-flag combinations, uncertainty plus severity, and vulnerable-population
markers.

Coordinate-framework grounding: increase monitoring density near
NormativeRegion boundaries — proximity to a constraint boundary
warrants heightened attention."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic that triggers risk escalation on dangerous
combinations of uncertainty, severity, and vulnerability."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DiagnosticHeuristic — hypothesis generation and pruning
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:DiagnosticHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnostic Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for diagnostic reasoning. Generates hypotheses broadly
then prunes using discriminating evidence.

Coordinate-framework grounding: maximise information gain per reasoning
step — choose tests and observations that most efficiently reduce
uncertainty about the entity's position in the coordinate space."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic for diagnostic reasoning that generates hypotheses
broadly and prunes by discriminating evidence."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CoordinationHeuristic — team and handoff management
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:CoordinationHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Coordination Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for care coordination and team management. Minimises
handoff count, maximises accountability continuity, and prefers known
competent teams.

Coordinate-framework grounding: minimise discontinuity in agent-assignment
dimensions — smooth agent transitions preserve contextual knowledge."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic for coordination that minimises handoffs and maximises
accountability continuity."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GovernanceHeuristic — escalation under uncertainty or novelty
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:GovernanceHeuristic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Governance Heuristic"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """A heuristic for governance-aware escalation. Requires human review
when confidence is low, novelty is high, explanation is weak, or
downstream risk is material.

Coordinate-framework grounding: escalate when the entity's position is
near the FormalisationFrontier or a NormativeRegion boundary — these
are the situations where heuristic defaults are least reliable."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A heuristic that triggers governance review under conditions of
low confidence, high novelty, or material downstream risk."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HeuristicPack — named, versionable collection of heuristics
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:HeuristicPack a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Heuristic Pack"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """A named, versionable collection of heuristics applicable to a domain,
service line, or regulatory context. A HeuristicPack bundles the heuristics
that apply to a particular operational situation — selecting the right pack
is the first step in heuristic reasoning.

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a HeuristicPack
is a specification of which heuristics apply and how they are configured
for a particular operational context."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A named, versionable collection of heuristics applicable to a
domain, service line, or regulatory context."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasMember — links HeuristicPack to its constituent heuristics
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasMember a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has member"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:HeuristicPack ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a HeuristicPack to its constituent heuristics. A pack contains
one or more heuristics that collectively cover the reasoning situation
for which the pack is designed."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# applicableToDomain — links heuristic or pack to a DomainIdentity
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:applicableToDomain a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "applicable to domain"@en ;
    rdfs:domain owl:Thing ;
    rdfs:range ontara-dom:DomainIdentity ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Heuristic or HeuristicPack to the DomainIdentity it applies to.
Both individual heuristics and entire packs can declare domain applicability.
Domain-specific heuristics override or supplement the default set for that
operational context."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# applicableToContext — links heuristic to activating reasoning contexts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:applicableToContext a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "applicable to context"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:ReasoningContext ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Heuristic to the ReasoningContext types that activate it.
A heuristic is applicable to a context when the context's properties
(goal type, decision mode, domain) match the heuristic's activation
criteria."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# overrides — links a heuristic to the one it overrides
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:overrides a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "overrides"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Heuristic to the Heuristic it overrides. Used when a domain-
or context-specific heuristic replaces a more general default.

Override events are recorded via PROV-O: prov:wasGeneratedBy on the
overriding heuristic links to the ReasoningActivity that decided the
override, preserving full provenance of which heuristic applied and
why it was chosen over the default."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasAuthorityBasis — links a heuristic to its justifying knowledge source
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasAuthorityBasis a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has authority basis"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:KnowledgeSource ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Heuristic to the KnowledgeSource that justifies its use.
A heuristic is not an arbitrary rule — it is grounded in evidence,
clinical guidelines, domain expertise, or regulatory requirements.
This property makes that grounding explicit and auditable."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasOrderingLogic — natural-language description of the heuristic's logic
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasOrderingLogic a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has ordering logic"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Heuristic ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Describes the ordering or selection logic of the Heuristic in natural
language. This is the human-readable rationale for why this heuristic
applies and how it ranks or selects among options. Required for
interpretability and governance audit."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 11: Decision Mode Routing (Phase 2 Step 2.2)
# =============================================================================
#
# Four named individuals of DecisionMode, mapping the Cynefin framework
# (Snowden & Boone) to the Ontara coordinate framework. Each mode corresponds
# to a region of the coordinate space defined by the entity's proximity to
# ClassificationRegion boundaries and the FormalisationFrontier.
#
# Two new properties: activatesComponent (routing mechanism) and
# transitionsTo (mode evolution).
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ClearMode — deterministic rules and checklists
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ClearMode a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                       ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "Clear Mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Deterministic rules, checklists, eligibility logic. Activates Tier 1
reasoning components. Appropriate when the situation is well-characterised
by existing rules and the correct response is known.

Coordinate-framework grounding: the entity's position is within a
well-mapped ClassificationRegion where deterministic paths exist.
Standard operating procedures fully specify the response."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ComplicatedMode — expert analysis and model-based trade-off
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ComplicatedMode a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "Complicated Mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Expert analysis, model-based trade-off, optimisation, simulation.
Activates Tier 1 + Tier 2 reasoning, possibly with structured probabilistic
support. The right answer exists but requires expertise to find.

Coordinate-framework grounding: the ClassificationRegion requires analysis
but admits tractable solutions. The entity's position can be precisely
located with sufficient expertise and modelling effort."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ComplexMode — probe-sense-respond and hypothesis portfolios
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ComplexMode a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "Complex Mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Probe-sense-respond, hypothesis portfolios, adaptive monitoring,
learning loops. Activates exploratory reasoning with Tier 1 safety
constraints as hard guards.

Coordinate-framework grounding: the entity's position is near or beyond
the FormalisationFrontier. Tractable solutions do not exist — the
system must probe the space, observe consequences, and adapt."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ChaoticMode — emergency stabilisation and hard constraint enforcement
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ChaoticMode a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "Chaotic Mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Emergency stabilisation, hard safety constraints, rapid escalation.
Activates only hard constraint enforcement and escalation. Act first,
sense second — the priority is stopping harm, not optimal decision-making.

Coordinate-framework grounding: the entity's position has crossed a
NormativeRegion boundary or is in crisis. Standard reasoning cannot
operate — immediate stabilisation is required before analysis resumes."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode transition assertions (transitionsTo)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:ClearMode ontara-rsn:transitionsTo ontara-rsn:ComplicatedMode ;
    rdfs:comment "When standard rules cannot resolve the situation and expert analysis is required."@en .

ontara-rsn:ComplicatedMode ontara-rsn:transitionsTo ontara-rsn:ComplexMode ;
    rdfs:comment "When analysis reveals the situation has no tractable solution and requires exploratory sensemaking."@en .

ontara-rsn:ChaoticMode ontara-rsn:transitionsTo ontara-rsn:ClearMode ;
    rdfs:comment "When crisis is stabilised and deterministic rules can resume normal operation."@en .

ontara-rsn:ComplexMode ontara-rsn:transitionsTo ontara-rsn:ComplicatedMode ;
    rdfs:comment "When exploratory probing has sufficiently characterised the situation for analytical approaches."@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# activatesComponent — links decision mode to component types it activates
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:activatesComponent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "activates component"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:ReasoningComponent ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a DecisionMode to the ReasoningComponent types it activates.
This is the routing mechanism: given a context's decision mode, the
system knows which components to engage. The routing is not exclusive
— modes may activate overlapping sets of components, with higher
modes activating all components of lower modes plus additional ones."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# transitionsTo — links one decision mode to another it may become
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:transitionsTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transitions to"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:DecisionMode ;
    rdfs:comment """Links one DecisionMode to another that the context may transition to.
A mode transition is a ReasoningActivity — the recognition that the
problem character has changed. Transitions may be triggered by new
evidence, escalation conditions, or stabilisation events."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# SECTION 12: Constraint Satisfaction Structures (Phase 2 Step 2.3)
# =============================================================================
#
# The algebraic structure governing how constraint values combine.
# Four named algebras cover the combination semantics for SoftConstraints
# (cost/preference semirings) and GradedRules (fuzzy logic, PSL).
#
# Parallels InterpretiveFrame (Section 5), which governs confidence value
# combination. CombinationAlgebra governs constraint value combination.
#
# New properties: hasCombinationAlgebra (functional), hasIdentityElement,
# composedWith (symmetric), hasPriority, hasTruthValueRange.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CombinationAlgebra — algebraic structure for combining constraint values
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Combination Algebra"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf bfo:IAO_0000030 ;
    rdfs:comment """The algebraic structure governing how constraint values combine. A
CombinationAlgebra specifies the combination operator (how values aggregate)
and the selection operator (how the best option is chosen).

BFO grounding: information content entity (IAO_0000030) — a specification
of combination semantics.

Parallels InterpretiveFrame (Section 5): InterpretiveFrame governs how
confidence values are interpreted and combined; CombinationAlgebra governs
how constraint values are combined to evaluate constraint satisfaction."""@en ;
    skos:definition """The algebraic structure governing how constraint values combine,
specifying combination and selection operators."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MinPlusSemiring — cost semiring for SoftConstraints
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:MinPlusSemiring a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:label "Min-Plus Semiring"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Cost semiring: combine via addition (total cost), select via minimum
(cheapest option). Standard algebra for SoftConstraint cost surfaces.

Formal structure: (ℝ⁺ ∪ {∞}, min, +, ∞, 0). The identity for addition
is 0 (zero cost); the identity for selection (min) is ∞ (no option is
worse than having no option at all)."""@en ;
    ontara-rsn:hasIdentityElement "0"^^xsd:string .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MaxTimesSemiring — preference semiring for SoftConstraints
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:MaxTimesSemiring a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:label "Max-Times Semiring"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Preference semiring: combine via multiplication (joint preference),
select via maximum (most preferred option). Alternative algebra for
SoftConstraint preference surfaces.

Formal structure: ([0,1], max, ×, 0, 1). The identity for multiplication
is 1 (full preference); the identity for selection (max) is 0 (no option
is less preferred than having no option at all)."""@en ;
    ontara-rsn:hasIdentityElement "1"^^xsd:string .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FuzzyMinMax — fuzzy logic operators for GradedRules
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:FuzzyMinMax a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:label "Fuzzy Min-Max"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Fuzzy logic combination: combine via min (conjunction) or max
(disjunction). Standard fuzzy operators for GradedRule truth-value surfaces.

Formal structure: ([0,1], max, min, 0, 1). Min corresponds to AND,
max corresponds to OR. The identity for min-conjunction is 1 (fully
true, so min with 1 leaves the other operand unchanged)."""@en ;
    ontara-rsn:hasIdentityElement "1 (for min-conjunction)"^^xsd:string .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PSLConvexOptimisation — Probabilistic Soft Logic for GradedRule composition
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:PSLConvexOptimisation a owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                    ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:label "PSL Convex Optimisation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Probabilistic Soft Logic combination: graded rules combine via convex
optimisation to find the most probable truth-value assignment. Standard
algebra for GradedRule composition across multiple interacting rules.

PSL relaxes logical rules to continuous truth values in [0,1] and finds
the most probable assignment by solving a convex optimisation problem.
There is no scalar identity element in the traditional sense — the
optimisation is over the joint assignment space."""@en ;
    ontara-rsn:hasIdentityElement "N/A (no scalar identity — convex optimisation over joint assignment)"^^xsd:string .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasCombinationAlgebra — links a Constraint to its combination algebra
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasCombinationAlgebra a owl:ObjectProperty ,
                                    owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has combination algebra"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Constraint to its CombinationAlgebra. Functional — each
constraint has exactly one combination algebra. This determines how
the constraint's values aggregate and how the best-satisfying option
is selected when multiple options are available."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasIdentityElement — the identity element of a CombinationAlgebra
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasIdentityElement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has identity element"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:CombinationAlgebra ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """The identity element of the CombinationAlgebra. String type to
accommodate "not applicable" for algebras such as PSLConvexOptimisation
where no scalar identity exists. For semirings, this is the identity
of the combination operator (addition or multiplication)."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# composedWith — two constraints interact and should be evaluated together
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:composedWith a owl:ObjectProperty ,
                           owl:SymmetricProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composed with"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:comment """Expresses that two Constraints interact and should be evaluated
together. Symmetric — if A is composedWith B, then B is composedWith A.
Composition may be necessary when constraints share variables, conflict,
or must be satisfied jointly for a decision to be valid."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasPriority — relative priority for constraint relaxation
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasPriority a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has priority"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:comment """Relative priority for constraint relaxation ordering. Higher integer
values take priority over lower values. When constraints conflict, lower-
priority constraints are relaxed first. HardConstraints should carry
higher priorities than SoftConstraints; governance obligations (aligned
as HardConstraints) should carry the highest priorities."""@en .


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hasTruthValueRange — documents the truth-value range for GradedRules
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

ontara-rsn:hasTruthValueRange a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has truth value range"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ontara-rsn:GradedRule ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """Documents the truth-value range of a GradedRule. Typically "[0,1]"
for standard fuzzy logic and PSL, but may differ for other graded
logics. String type to accommodate non-standard ranges.

Used by the reasoning runtime to validate that GradedRule outputs
are within the declared range before they are used in combination."""@en .


# =============================================================================
# End of Phase 2 Steps 2.1, 2.2, and 2.3
# =============================================================================
#
# Class count: 34 (26 Phase 1 + 8 Phase 2)
#   Phase 2 additions:
#     Step 2.1: GoalOrderingHeuristic, ResourceHeuristic, RiskHeuristic,
#               DiagnosticHeuristic, CoordinationHeuristic, GovernanceHeuristic,
#               HeuristicPack
#     Step 2.3: CombinationAlgebra
#
# Named individuals: 11 (3 Phase 1 + 4 Step 2.2 + 4 Step 2.3)
#   Phase 1: ProbabilityFrame, FuzzyMembershipFrame, PreferenceWeightFrame
#   Step 2.2: ClearMode, ComplicatedMode, ComplexMode, ChaoticMode
#   Step 2.3: MinPlusSemiring, MaxTimesSemiring, FuzzyMinMax, PSLConvexOptimisation
#
# Object property count: 21 (15 Phase 1 + 6 Phase 2)
#   Step 2.1: hasMember, applicableToDomain, applicableToContext,
#             overrides, hasAuthorityBasis
#   Step 2.2: activatesComponent, transitionsTo
#
# Datatype property count: 7 (4 Phase 1 + 3 Phase 2)
#   Step 2.1: hasOrderingLogic
#   Step 2.3: hasIdentityElement, hasPriority, hasTruthValueRange
#   (Note: hasIdentityElement is declared as both datatype property and
#    used in named individual assertions — confirm it is a DatatypeProperty)
#
# =============================================================================
